---
title: Search
summary: One search engine that runs in two places. Ship a static index to an on-device worker, or query it on the server, with a no-JS fallback page in both cases.
related:
  - content/remotes
  - content/emit
  - content/shell
---

The site layer builds a search index from the **section documents** of your collections, meaning the prose split at each heading, so a hit lands on the exact section rather than only the page. You choose where the query runs: on the server, or on the visitor's device against a static index. The search on this site works the second way, with a worker over a prerendered file, so a query costs no server round trip.

The static index is usually the better choice for a prerendered site, because it costs nothing to serve and works offline. Query on the server instead when the corpus is private, very large, or changes per request.

## Two paths, one engine

::: code-group

```ts [static index (recommended)]
// src/routes/docs/search.json/+server.ts — the prerendered index
import { docs } from '$lib/site.server';
export const prerender = true;
export const GET = docs.emit.search();
```

```ts [server query]
// src/lib/site.remote.ts — when the index shouldn't ship
import { remotes } from '@ogygia/content/server';
import { docs } from './site.server';
export const { search } = remotes(docs, { base: '/docs' });
```

:::

Both paths use the same engine (`docs.search`), the same section documents and the same ranking; only the place where the query runs differs.

## The palette

`<Search>` is the ⌘K palette, an [island](/docs/regions/client-islands) that loads on demand:

```svelte
<script>
	import { Search } from '@ogygia/content';
</script>

<Search base="/docs" placeholder="Search the docs" />
```

The server renders only the trigger, which is a real link to the fallback page. On the first sign of _intent_ (⌘K, `/` or a click) it loads the palette and starts an [Orama](https://orama.com) worker that fetches and indexes the static file **off the main thread**. Ranking highlights matched terms and boosts title matches. No server ever sees a query, and nothing heavy loads for a visitor who never searches.

Orama is an **optional peer dependency** that the scaffold installs and that loads only on the search path:

::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npm i @orama/orama
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm add @orama/orama
```

:::

## The server path, rendered

If you query over the wire instead, the page takes a few lines and returns the same hits in the same shape:

```svelte
<script>
	import { search } from '$lib/site.remote';
	let q = $state('');
</script>

<input bind:value={q} />
{#each await search(q) as hit}
	<a href={hit.href}>{hit.title} — {hit.excerpt}</a>
{/each}
```

## The no-JS fallback

Search should work without JavaScript. The `<Search>` trigger is a real `<a href="/docs/search">`, which opens the palette when JS is available and otherwise follows the link to a plain results page that you render from a server load:

```ts title=src/routes/docs/search/+page.server.ts
import { docs } from '$lib/site.server';
export const load = async ({ url }) => ({
	q: url.searchParams.get('q') ?? '',
	hits: await docs.search(url.searchParams.get('q') ?? '', { base: '/docs' })
});
```

```svelte
<script>
	import { SearchPage } from '@ogygia/content';
	let { data } = $props();
</script>

<SearchPage q={data.q} hits={data.hits} />
```

The fallback uses the same index and ranking with no client JS at all. This site has one, which you can see by opening `/docs/search` directly.

## Scoping and swapping

`docs.search(q, { in: [collection] })` narrows the query to one collection, which is useful when a site carries several (docs and a blog, say) and a search box should cover only one. The static index includes every collection, and the worker filters by scope.

The engine itself is a **contract rather than a fixed dependency**. Orama is the default, and `site({ search: { engine } })` replaces it with anything that can build and query the section documents, such as a hosted service, a different library or a test double.

::: details What a hit looks like
`SearchHit` is plain data, ready to render:

| field     | meaning                                                                    |
| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `href`    | deep link, baked for `base`; lands on the heading of the matched _section_ |
| `title`   | the page title                                                             |
| `section` | the nav group / top-level section label                                    |
| `heading` | the heading of the matched section                                         |
| `excerpt` | the matched prose, with `<mark>` around matched terms                      |
| `score`   | the rank from the engine, with title matches boosted                       |
:::

## Do / don't

- **Do** ship the static index for a prerendered site. An on-device worker is faster to serve, works offline, and the server never sees a keystroke.
- **Do** keep the fallback page. It is one server load, and it keeps search working when JS is off or has not loaded yet.
- **Don't** create a `search` remote _and_ ship the index for the same search box. Pick one path: the index is for the worker, and the remote is for server queries.
- **Don't** build your own index from the raw bodies. `docs.emit.search()` splits by section, which is what lets a hit link straight to the right heading.
